### =============================================================
### OWASP A07 – Identification and Authentication Failures
### =============================================================
### Three problems:
###   (a) No token expiry
###   (b) No issuer/audience check → token confusion
###   (c) Known secret → anyone can sign tokens (see the A02 file)
### =============================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5218


### --- (a) Log in – get a token ---
# Note the token for the next requests
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "password"
}

###

### --- (a) An expired token is still accepted ---
# This JWT expired in 2020 (exp: 1577836800 = 2020-01-01).
# A correct system would reject it with 401.
# Actual: 200 OK – the expiry date is ignored.
#
# Payload (decoded, claim names shortened):
#   { "exp": 1577836800, "nameidentifier": "2", "name": "alice", "role": "customer" }
#
# Real-world scenario: an attacker steals a token. Even after a password reset,
# or years later, the token still works.
@expiredToken = eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..b2gWZCl3BQ4Mu1WQUWkmrYTPRLkVPAs-v2vdKpnhjkA

GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/my
Authorization: Bearer {{expiredToken}}

###

### --- (b) Token confusion: a token issued for a different service ---
# Tokens from any other service (e.g. an internal HR app, a CI system) are
# accepted by the Aviato API, as long as the signing secret matches.
#
# In a microservice architecture, one compromised service can then attack
# every other service – without credentials of its own.
#
# This token was issued for "issuer=hr-system, audience=internal-tools",
# but carries userId=1 (admin) and role=admin:
@foreignServiceToken = eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..o_YtXQgzNpX1VyBvPP3RaaEmRcSw0f1cDP1OeZd0GqU

GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/my
Authorization: Bearer {{foreignServiceToken}}
